---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatXml from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatXml.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="xml-validator">
  <DevFormatXml client:load mode="validate" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Well-formed is not the same as valid</h2>
    <p>
      XML has two levels of correctness, and confusing them wastes time. Well-formed means the document follows
      XML&rsquo;s syntax: one root element, every tag closed and correctly nested, attributes quoted, entities
      defined, no stray <code>&amp;</code> or <code>&lt;</code>. Valid means it also conforms to a specific
      schema: a DTD, XSD or RELAX NG grammar that says which elements may appear where.
    </p>
    <p>
      This XML validator checks well-formedness, which catches most real failures, with the browser&rsquo;s own
      parser. Schema validation needs the schema file and a validating processor, which belongs in a build
      pipeline.
    </p>

    <h2>XML errors and what they mean</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Mismatched tag</td><td>A closing tag does not match the opening tag. XML is case-sensitive.</td><td><code>&lt;Item&gt;</code> must close with <code>&lt;/Item&gt;</code>, never <code>&lt;/item&gt;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Premature end of data</td><td>The document ends with elements still open</td><td>Find the last unclosed tag, usually near a truncated copy-paste</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Entity not defined</td><td>An HTML entity such as <code>&amp;nbsp;</code></td><td>Use a numeric reference: <code>&amp;#160;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Not well-formed / invalid token</td><td>A bare <code>&amp;</code> or <code>&lt;</code> in text</td><td>Escape them as <code>&amp;amp;</code> and <code>&amp;lt;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Junk after document element</td><td>More than one root element</td><td>Wrap everything in a single container element</td></tr>
        <tr><td>XML declaration allowed only at the start</td><td>A blank line, a BOM or a comment before <code>&lt;?xml</code></td><td>Delete whatever precedes it, including invisible bytes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Prefix not bound</td><td>A namespace prefix used without a declaration</td><td>Add the matching <code>xmlns:prefix="…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duplicate attribute</td><td>The same attribute set twice on one element</td><td>Remove the repeat</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Specification mandate value for attribute</td><td>A boolean-style attribute with no value</td><td>XML requires <code>name="value"</code> for every attribute</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Why the ampersand causes so much trouble</h2>
    <p>
      A raw <code>&amp;</code> is the most common cause of an invalid XML document, and it almost always comes
      from data, not hand-written markup: a company name (&ldquo;Smith &amp; Sons&rdquo;), a URL with query
      parameters (<code>?a=1&amp;b=2</code>), or a value already HTML-escaped once. When a value goes into a
      template without escaping, every ampersand in the data becomes a syntax error. Escape when you insert
      the value, not afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2>Only five entities exist</h2>
    <p>
      XML predefines only <code>&amp;amp;</code>, <code>&amp;lt;</code>, <code>&amp;gt;</code>,
      <code>&amp;quot;</code> and <code>&amp;apos;</code>. HTML&rsquo;s roughly two thousand named entities are
      not available unless a DTD declares them, so documents copied from HTML fail here. You can still write
      any character as a numeric reference, such as <code>&amp;#8212;</code> or <code>&amp;#x2014;</code> for an
      em dash, or type it directly in a UTF-8 document.
    </p>

    <h2>After it passes</h2>
    <p>
      Well-formedness is the minimum. It does not show whether required elements are present, whether values are
      in range, or whether the receiving service accepts the document. If you have an XSD, validate against it
      in your build. When you debug an integration, a formatted, well-formed document is where you start to
      read the content; for large documents, use the <a href="/xml-viewer">tree viewer</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
